&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;Impairment of Long-Lived Assets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; text-indent: .45in; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;PRMM reviews property, plant, and equipment for impairment whenever events or changes in business circumstances indicate the carrying value of the assets may not be recoverable. Impairment is indicated when the undiscounted cash flows estimated to be generated by those assets are less than the assets&amp;#8217; carrying value. If this occurs, an impairment loss is recognized for the difference between the fair value and carrying value. Factors that indicate potential impairment include a significant decrease in the market value of the asset, operating or cash flow losses associated with the use of the asset, and a significant change in the asset&amp;#8217;s physical condition or use.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 6.0pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: .45in; page-break-after: avoid;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;Simultaneously with PRMM&amp;#8217;s review of its property, plant, and equipment for impairment, PRMM evaluates whether an abandonment has occurred. Abandonment occurs either when a business terminates its operations or an asset is no longer profitable to operate. When the act of abandonment occurs, PRMM would write off the asset balance and any associated accumulated depreciation and record an impairment loss as needed.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 6.0pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: .45in; page-break-after: avoid;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;PRMM did not have any impairment or abandonment of assets during the years ended December&amp;#160;31, 2025 or 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; text-indent: .45in; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;PRMM records asset retirement obligations (&amp;#8220;AROs&amp;#8221;) at fair value in the period in which it has a legal obligation, whether by government action or contractual arrangement, to incur these costs and can make a reasonable estimate of the fair value of the liability. PRMM cannot currently estimate the fair value for certain AROs primarily because it cannot estimate settlement dates (or ranges of dates) associated with its assets. These AROs include hazardous materials disposal (such as petroleum manufacturing by-products) and removal or dismantlement requirements associated with the closure of the pipeline system, including the demolition or removal of certain major buildings, tanks, pipelines, or other equipment. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; text-indent: .5in; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;PRMM&amp;#8217;s results of operations are included in the consolidated federal income tax returns filed by Par. PRMM has a tax sharing agreement with Par and the Affiliates, but for the purposes of this filing has presented its income tax provision as if it filed a separate return.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; text-indent: .5in; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;PRMM uses the asset and liability method of accounting for income taxes. Under the asset and liability method, deferred tax assets and liabilities are recognized for the future tax consequences attributable to differences between the financial statement carrying amounts of existing assets and liabilities and their respective tax bases and net operating loss ("NOL") and tax credit carry forwards. Deferred tax assets and liabilities are measured using enacted income tax rates expected to apply to taxable income in the years in which those differences are expected to be recovered or settled. The effect on deferred tax assets and liabilities of a change in income tax rates is recognized in the results of operations in the period that includes the enactment date. The realizability of deferred tax assets is evaluated each reporting period based on a &amp;#8220;more likely than not&amp;#8221; standard and, to the extent this threshold is not met, a valuation allowance is recorded&lt;strong&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;Revenue Recognition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; text-indent: .5in; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;PRMM&amp;#8217;s revenues are primarily associated with tariff fees collected in exchange for transportation services provided to customers. Most of PRMM's revenues represent transactions with PML, the intercompany shipper. PRMM&amp;#8217;s contract with its customers is the tariff filed with FERC that states the terms of the invoice, including the crude weight, receipt and delivery location, pipeline loss allowance, and rate of each crude transported. Payments from customers are generally due in full on or before 10 days of invoice date. As such, PRMM has no significant financing element to its revenues and has no product returns and refunds.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;Cost Classifications&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; text-indent: .5in; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;Operations and Maintenance expenses include the direct operating expenses related to the transportation of crude on the Silvertip Pipeline system, including oil losses and shortages (or pipeline gains, in some periods).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; text-indent: .5in; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;General expenses include costs of labor, depreciation, property taxes, and other expenses not considered directly related to operations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="text-align: justify; line-height: normal; page-break-after: avoid; mso-layout-grid-align: none; text-autospace: none; margin: 8.1pt 0in 6.0pt 0in;"&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;Recent Accounting Pronouncements&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 6.0pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: .5in; page-break-after: avoid;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;On November 4, 2024, the FASB issued ASU 2024-03, Income Statement&amp;#8212;Reporting Comprehensive Income&amp;#8212;Expense Disaggregation Disclosures. This ASU requires companies to disclose, in the notes to financial statements, specified information about certain costs and expenses. The amendments in this ASU are effective for annual reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2026, and interim reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2027. Early adoption is permitted. While this ASU primarily relates to GAAP filings, we are also currently evaluating the impact of adopting the new guidance on FERC filings subsequent to the effective date. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 6.0pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: .5in; page-break-after: avoid;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif; mso-font-kerning: 0pt;"&gt;On September 18, 2025, the FASB Issued ASU 2025-06, Intangibles&amp;#8212;Goodwill and Other&amp;#8212;Internal-Use Software (Subtopic 350-40): Targeted Improvements to the Accounting for Internal-Use Software. This ASU requires companies to disclose all software costs capitalized under ASC 350-40 in accordance with property, plant and equipment disclosure requirements under ASC 360-10, The amendments in this ASU are effective for annual and interim reporting periods beginning after December 15, 2027. Early adoption is permitted. We are currently evaluating the impact of adopting the new guidance on filings subsequent to the effective date.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 6.0pt; text-align: justify; page-break-after: avoid;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;Note 3&amp;#8212;Revenue Recognition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 6.0pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: .5in;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;At December 31, 2025 and 2024, receivables from contracts with affiliates were $1.6 million and $1.4 million, respectively. At December 31, 2025 and 2024, there were no third-party balances from transportation contracts with third parties pending receipt.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p class="MsoNormal" style="margin-bottom: 6.0pt; text-align: justify; text-indent: .5in; page-break-after: avoid;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 9.0pt; line-height: 107%; font-family: 'Arial',sans-serif;"&gt;The following table provides information about disaggregated revenue by service and counterparty (in thousands):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
